<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alvars</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">6th–9th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century">6th century</a> · Also known as Azhwars</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Alvars were twelve Tamil poet-saints devoted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a> who lived in south <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> between about the 6th and 9th centuries, among them the woman poet Andal. Their emotional hymns of devotion, composed in Tamil and sung in temples, were gathered by Nathamuni in the 10th century as the Nalayira Divya Prabandham. With the Shaiva <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayanars">Nayanars</a> they led the bhakti movement that revived <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hinduism</a> in the Tamil country.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alvars.jpg" alt="Alvars" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ssriram mt · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thiruthankal_temple_(7).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
